<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chengdu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chengdu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the fertile Chengdu plain. It has been the political centre of the region since the ancient state of Shu and was the capital of several regional regimes, including Shu Han in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> and the Former and Later Shu in the 10th century. The nearby Dujiangyan irrigation system, built in the 3rd century BCE, made the plain one of China's richest farming areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chengdu.jpg" alt="Chengdu" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FISU · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E7%9A%84%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E5%B8%82%E5%A4%A9%E9%99%85%E7%BA%BF_Chengdu_skyline_with_snow_capped_mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shi Dakai, whose family Wei and Qin had killed, led his army west and was executed by slow slicing near <b>Chengdu</b> in 1863.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Xizong then abandoned Chang'an for <b>Chengdu</b>, and Huang entered the capital and declared himself emperor of a new state of Qi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Shu emerged in the <b>Chengdu</b> plain from a long local tradition: the Sanxingdui culture, with its giant bronze masks and standing figure, and the later Jinsha site in Chengdu.</blockquote><a href="/state/ancient-shu">Ancient Shu</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death his son Li Xiong took <b>Chengdu</b> in 304, styling himself King of Chengdu, and in 306 proclaimed himself emperor of Cheng.</blockquote><a href="/state/cheng-han">Cheng Han</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Xin collapsed he seized the region, took the title King of Shu and in 25 proclaimed himself emperor of Chengjia, with his capital at <b>Chengdu</b>, the same year Liu Xiu restored the Han.</blockquote><a href="/state/chengjia">Chengjia</a></li><li><blockquote>Wang Jian, a soldier from Henan who had served in the palace army, seized <b>Chengdu</b> in 891 and became military governor of the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a></li></ul></section>
